::U6F_L1_01::Substance use is {=a broad term that does not automatically mean misuse ~always addiction ~always illegal ~always overdose}

::U6F_L1_02::Substance misuse includes {=taking another person's prescription ~following one's directions ~seeking help ~safe storage}

::U6F_L1_03::Tolerance means {=reduced response after repeated exposure ~increased safety ~recovery ~refusal}

::U6F_L1_04::Dependence may involve {=adaptation and withdrawal ~only peer pressure ~a legal status ~a marketing claim}

::U6F_L1_05::A risk factor {=increases vulnerability without guaranteeing an outcome ~proves a diagnosis ~cannot change ~is always genetic}

::U6F_L1_06::A protective factor is {=a supportive trusted adult ~peer pressure ~poor coping ~misinformation}

::U6F_L1_07::Which is valid reasoning? {=One warning sign does not diagnose a disorder; use qualified assessment ~One sign proves addiction ~Risk guarantees outcome ~Stigma improves care}

::U6F_L2_01::Alcohol is a {=central-nervous-system depressant ~vitamin ~antibiotic ~stimulant only}

::U6F_L2_02::A U.S. standard drink contains about {=14 grams of pure alcohol ~14 grams of sugar ~one container of any size ~zero ethanol}

::U6F_L2_03::BAC means {=blood alcohol concentration ~a personal safe limit ~beverage alcohol color ~breathing alert code}

::U6F_L2_04::Coffee or a cold shower {=does not quickly remove alcohol; time is required ~guarantees sobriety ~lowers BAC to zero ~prevents impairment}

::U6F_L2_05::Impaired driving risk involves {=reduced judgment, reaction time, coordination, and attention ~only beverage taste ~only road knowledge ~no brain functions}

::U6F_L2_06::Alcohol-overdose signs include {=unresponsiveness and abnormal breathing ~better coordination ~mild thirst only ~faster speech only}

::U6F_L2_07::Underage abstinence is protective because it {=avoids developmental, legal, safety, and goal risks ~guarantees popularity ~replaces help-seeking ~makes driving safer after use}

::U6F_L2_08::The best response to suspected alcohol overdose is {=call emergency services, monitor, follow directions, and stay ~let the person sleep alone ~give coffee ~make the person walk}

::U6F_L3_01::Nicotine can {=produce dependence and withdrawal ~guarantee focus ~remove lung risk ~reverse THC}

::U6F_L3_02::Vape emissions are {=aerosol, not harmless water vapor ~oxygen only ~always nicotine-free ~the same as clean air}

::U6F_L3_03::Dual use means {=using cigarettes and e-cigarettes ~quitting ~using two flavors ~safe use}

::U6F_L3_04::THC may impair {=attention, memory, reaction time, coordination, and judgment ~only price recognition ~only packaging choices ~no safety skill}

::U6F_L3_05::A delayed effect can increase risk when {=a person takes more before the first amount fully affects them ~it eliminates impairment ~it verifies potency ~it guarantees medical use}

::U6F_L3_06::“Natural” or “legal” {=does not automatically mean harmless ~guarantees safe driving ~prevents dependence ~proves a marketing claim}

::U6F_L3_07::Social-media visibility {=does not prove most people use a product ~is representative prevalence data ~proves safety ~eliminates bias}

::U6F_L3_08::A marketing claim should be evaluated by {=checking composition, evidence, missing context, and source credibility ~counting likes ~testing the product ~trusting colors}

::U6F_L4_01::Appropriate prescription use requires {=right person, medicine, dose, time/route, and purpose ~sharing ~changing dose ~online purchase}

::U6F_L4_02::Opioids can cause {=respiratory depression ~guaranteed alertness ~immunity ~faster breathing only}

::U6F_L4_03::Fentanyl is {=a very potent synthetic opioid ~an OTC vitamin ~a stimulant antidote ~a harmless filler}

::U6F_L4_04::A counterfeit pill {=may copy a legitimate pill while containing unknown substances ~is verified by appearance ~always comes from a pharmacy ~has predictable potency}

::U6F_L4_05::Mixing depressants {=can compound sedation and breathing suppression ~always balances effects ~eliminates overdose ~is safe if doses are small}

::U6F_L4_06::Unresponsiveness with slow, gurgling breathing requires {=immediate emergency services ~a cold shower ~waiting ~food}

::U6F_L4_07::Naloxone's boundary is that it {=does not replace emergency care and monitoring ~reverses every substance ~works permanently ~verifies a pill}

::U6F_L4_08::Secure storage and take-back disposal {=reduce unauthorized access and misuse ~increase potency ~replace labels ~allow sharing}

::U6F_L4_09::Stigma can {=discourage timely help-seeking ~verify a diagnosis ~reverse overdose ~improve breathing}

::U6F_L5_01::A protective factor {=reduces risk or supports healthy action ~guarantees no difficulty ~is the same as peer pressure ~proves a diagnosis}

::U6F_L5_02::An assertive refusal includes {=clear no, reason/value, alternative, exit, and support ~a vague maybe ~a long debate ~personal disclosure}

::U6F_L5_03::An impaired-ride exit plan should {=refuse the ride and activate a safe transport backup ~depend on the driver's promise ~wait in the car ~hide the concern}

::U6F_L5_04::In suspected overdose, accurate information for responders includes {=location, observed signs, and known substances ~only the person's name ~a rumor ~no details}

::U6F_L5_05::A recovery position is used {=when appropriate while monitoring and awaiting help ~instead of calling ~to force vomiting ~to end observation}

::U6F_L5_06::Which resource matches an immediate life threat? {=Emergency services ~an influencer ~a seller ~a general homework forum}

::U6F_L5_07::A credible ongoing-care resource is {=a licensed medical or behavioral-health professional ~an anonymous account ~a product advertisement ~a peer rumor}

::U6F_L5_08::A complete action plan includes {=trigger, action, first resource, backup, barrier solution, and goal link ~only a goal ~only a phone number ~only a refusal word}
